ner book handy here somewhere and  they never leave it because they've  never they never feel comfortable they  don't feel they've learned everything in  the beginner book the beginner book  doesn't matter  the beginner book is like it's like that  step into the swimming pool and then you  got to start swimming  you got to start swimming so the biggest  mistake that people make  is that they stay with their beginner  material  either you know for example  i looked up i looked beginner check  courses on the internet just to see what  was there  and there are courses offered at ubc  university of british columbia here in  vancouver and they have a three-month  beginner course and a three month that's  a beginner one then they have a three  month beginner two intermediate one  course so that was six months and you're  still just beginner to intermediate one  and they do the same for dutch i mean  dutch dutch is so similar to english  and and yet six months  and you're still a beginner too or an  intermediate one no  you have to and it doesn't matter  whether you can use the language it  doesn't matter whether you can speak yet  or write yet what matters is how much  can you understand  how many words do you have how familiar  are you with the language to what extent  is the language starting to become a  part of your  the brain whatever so  the big mistake people make is they stay  with the beginner book for far too long  within a month or two or three depending  on the language you've got to get into  the real  language okay  and  to ease your way in you have now got  resources on the web with online  dictionaries and i hesitate to mention  link again because people claim that i'm  always touting linkedin that's what i  use it works for me  so there's my advice  don't stay with the beginner material  the sooner you get into the real stuff  the faster you will learn the objective  is not to fully understand something  after one or two months the objective is  to unders